In ordinary Moth code, `--` starts a single-line comment.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- Explain why this value exists.
    retry_count = 3
]

Everything after `--` on that source-code line is comment text.

### Template and moth template text

Template bodies are content, not ordinary code-token context.

Inside a template body, `--` is ordinary body text. A moth template `.mtf` file is
the body of an implicit compile-time Markdown template, so `--` is text there
too.

Use template directives such as `$note` or `$todo` when template-authored
content should be ignored.

### Naming conventions

- Variables and functions use `regular_snake_case`.
- Structs, choices, type aliases and generic parameters use `PascalCase`.
- Trait names use all-caps identifiers such as `DISPLAY_TEXT`.
- Names should describe their role rather than their storage or implementation detail.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    retry_count = 3

    load_profile |user_id String| -> String:
        return "profile"
    ;

    UserProfile = |
        display_name String,
    |
]

### Name collisions

A visible name cannot be redeclared while it remains in scope.

The complete no-shadowing contract is documented by
@../bindings/#shadowing (Values and bindings). Dependency aliases and module API
names have additional collision rules in the Packages documentation.

### Configuration names

The accepted queued project and page directives use the `$` marker: `$config`,
`$project`, `$html_builder` and `$page`. There is no accepted `config:` block or
`#Config` qualifier.
Directive spellings do not reserve ordinary identifiers, so `config`, `Config`,
`project` and `html_builder` remain ordinary names outside directive syntax.
The file `config.moth` and the dependency root `@project` retain their separate
path and package semantics.

**Current support and cutover.** The Alpha compiler still supports `#Config` and
currently reserves its case-insensitive, leading-underscore keyword-shadow names.
Identifier reclamation is pending until the cutover to `$config`; code targeting
the current compiler must follow its current reservation policy.

`import` is not a keyword. `Import` is an ordinary valid PascalCase user name:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Import = |
        source String,
    |
]

Ordinary dependency clauses do not use an `import` keyword.

`block`, `group` and `region` are not keywords. They follow ordinary identifier policy. In executable statement position, each corresponding `name:` header has the same reserved declared-region shape as any other valid identifier.
